+## 18. (NEW) G16 - the injury contract is not ready for a real source (Step 4.5)
+
+`getPlayerInjuries` is built and honest, but only because no injury source exists. Before ANY injury data is integrated the contract needs:
+per-record provenance (`InjuryRecord` has no `data_origin`/`dataset_version`/`imported_at`, so sample and real records could not be told apart),
+a defined record order (the endpoint uses `start_date`, `expected_return`, `id` ASC with NULLs last: a decision, not a contract rule),
+a rule that `HAS_RECORDS` implies at least one row (today a stored `HAS_RECORDS` without rows is reported as stored, `records: []`), a logical key for
+`injury_records` (rows are not collapsed), and an `injury` entry in the source registry. All moot today: the database holds only `NO_SOURCE_AVAILABLE`
+rows and zero `injury_records`. HAS_RECORDS behaviour was verified on synthetic rows in throwaway database copies only.
+
+## 19. (NOTE) Step 4.5 test-quality findings (fixed, no product bug)
+
+The first mutation run left two mutants alive: a `checked_at = now()` mutation (the original assertion only held when the database timestamp
+and the request fell in different seconds, i.e. it was time-dependent) and an ordering mutation that coincidentally matched the test data. Both were
+test weaknesses, not endpoint bugs; fixed with a deterministic fixed-timestamp test and tie-break data in which `expected_return` order disagrees with
+`id` order. The 4.4 and 4.5 endpoints still duplicate a ~7-line id/health guard (`getPlayer`, `getPlayerMarketValue`, `getPlayerInjuries`); folding it
+into one helper is an optional later cleanup, deliberately not done here (no unrelated refactors).
